Chapter: DtlsAppLoopbackTest
  cites Net chapter DtlsEndpoint
  cites Encode chapter Dtls
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 Application data over an AUTHENTICATED DTLS 1.3 handshake, end to end, with
 no network. The handshake is the authenticated loopback's; what this adds
 is the two rungs above it -- the server flight and both Finished messages
 protected at the handshake epoch (2), and then application data at epoch 3
 under the application traffic keys.

 The record layer is oracle-validated (codex/test/dtls-record checks
 dtls-seal / dtls-open against an independent implementation); the key
 schedule is the generic TLS 1.3 one the RFC 8448 vectors gate. What this
 test cannot prove is interop, because no third-party DTLS peer is in the
 loop -- so the negative control is the tamper: a flipped ciphertext byte
 must yield NO plaintext, or the AEAD is decoration.

 The whole exchange runs in one let-chain rather than as top-level bindings:
 each step allocates, there is no collector, and a top-level binding is
 re-evaluated at every mention -- so mentioning the handshake from six
 assertions runs it six times and exhausts the heap. Bound once, used once.

